<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.7.1: Factoring Strategies for Polynomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01690</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.7.1: Factoring Strategies for Polynomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>890afa41-d59e-4329-9503-839a0fd88203</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
<para>This warm up refreshes and reviews all the factoring strategies learned in this unit. Students will remember that
  each strategy is useful for a specific type of polynomial. In this activity, they will work in pairs to recall the
  ideal type of polynomial for each strategy learned so far.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of 2. Have each pair of students complete the activity questions together.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Working with a partner, list as many strategies as you can for factoring polynomials. </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<list>
  <item>Factoring by finding the GCF of an expression and using reverse distribution</item>
  <item>Factoring using grouping</item>
  <item>Factoring trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\) (undo FOIL)</item>
  <item>Factoring trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using trial and error</item>
  <item>Factoring trinomials using the “\(ac\)” method</item>
  <item>Factoring using substitution</item>
  <item>Factoring the difference of squares</item>
  <item>Factoring perfect square trinomials</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Work with your partner to write a sample polynomial that would be fitting for each strategy listed. </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>

    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Factoring Strategy
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Sample Polynomial
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Factoring by finding the GCF of an expression and using reverse distribution
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(14a + 8ab + 12\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Factoring using grouping
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(x^2 + 2x − 5x − 10\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Factoring trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\) (undo FOIL)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(t^2 + 14t + 24\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Factoring trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using trial and error
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(18x^2 − 3xy − 10y^2\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Factoring trinomials using the “\(ac\)” method
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(16x^2 − 32x + 12\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Factoring using substitution
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \((x−2)^2 + 7(x−2) + 12\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Factoring the difference of squares
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(81y^2 − 1\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Factoring perfect square trinomials
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(25v^2 + 20v + 4\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students:</para>
<list>
  <item> Are there any strategies that you are not sure when to use? Address their questions in regard to when to use any
    strategies mentioned. </item>
  <item> Do you have a favorite strategy? Explain. (Sample answer: the “\(ac\)” method because it works on any
    trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx +c\).) </item>
  <item> Do you have a least favorite strategy? Explain. </item>
</list></section>
